# No links to private Git repositories

Do not add markdown links, bare URLs, or clickable references to **private** Git hosting URLs (GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, Azure DevOps, etc.) in documentation, skills, rules, comments, or chat replies.

Readers without access get broken links; private URLs also leak org/repo structure.

## What counts as private

- Repositories marked private on the host, or known to be private in this workspace
- Any repo you are not certain is **public** — treat as private and do not link

Known private repo in this workspace: **browser-bookmarks-sync** (`basvdberg/browser-bookmarks-sync`).

## Instead of a URL

| Situation | Do this |
|-----------|---------|
| Repo is private | Specify name only |
| Repo is public | Full `https://` link is fine |
| User needs the remote | Say they can open it in Git or their IDE; do not paste the private URL unless they explicitly ask |

## Examples

```markdown
<!-- BAD: private repo with clickable link -->
[Browser bookmarks sync](https://github.com/basvdberg/browser-bookmarks-sync)

<!-- GOOD: name only -->
Browser bookmarks sync

<!-- GOOD: public repo -->
[Data Engineering 2026](https://github.com/basvdberg/data-engineering-2026)
```

When updating **Related repositories** in project structure blocks or `project-structure-external.json`, **omit** private repositories entirely (use `"private": ["repo-slug"]` to exclude them from auto-generated lists).
